  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "What is the first term that exceeds $58\\,000$ in the following geometric sequence?\n\n$$\n2,\\,8,\\,32,\\,128,\\,\u2026\n$$\n\nThe first term that exceeds $58\\,000$ is the\n{{field-1}}th term.",
  "worked_solution": "The first term of the sequence is ${a}_{1}=2$ and the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{8}{2}=4.\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the sequence can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =2\\cdot {4}^{n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want the $n$th term to be greater than $58\\,000.$\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & >58\\,000 \\\\ 2\\cdot {4}^{n-1} & >58\\,000 \\\\ {4}^{n-1} & >29\\,000 \\\\ \\operatorname{log}\u2061({4}^{n-1}) & >\\operatorname{log}\u2061(29\\,000) \\\\ (n-1)\\operatorname{log}\u2061(4) & >\\operatorname{log}\u2061(29\\,000) \\\\ n-1 & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}\u2061(29\\,000)}{\\operatorname{log}\u2061(4)} \\\\ n & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}\u2061(29\\,000)}{\\operatorname{log}\u2061(4)}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{log}\u2061(29\\,000)}{\\operatorname{log}\u2061(4)}+1\u22488.412.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $9$th term is the first term of the sequence that exceeds $58\\,000.$",
